@prefix : <#> .
@prefix schema: <http://schema.org/> .

<> a schema:HowTo ;
    schema:name "Verifying SPARQL Workbench Sample Queries Against a Live-Loaded Named Graph"@en ;
    schema:description "2026-08-15: user loaded the rdf-1-2-support-in-virtuoso-claude_sonnet_5-1.ttl companion RDF to URIBurner and asked to verify the SPARQL Workbench's 19 sample queries. Two distinct classes of defect were found and fixed/documented."@en ;
    schema:step :step1, :step2, :step3, :step4, :step5, :step6, :step7, :step8, :step9, :step10, :step11, :step12, :step13 .

:step1 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    schema:name "Re-violated the existing absolute-prefix-IRI rule (Step 50 / sparql-absolute-prefix-iri.ttl) when converting Turtle examples to live SPARQL"@en ;
    schema:text "12 Turtle examples verbatim from the post used PREFIX : <#> (a bare hash, correct for illustrative Turtle since the post's own base is understood from context). When those were wrapped in INSERT DATA/SELECT for the SPARQL Workbench, the <#> prefix was carried over unchanged. Empirical test: SELECT (<< :alice :worksFor :AcmeCorp >> AS ?statement) WHERE {} against the live endpoint returned subject/predicate/object values as bare '#alice' strings, not real dereferenceable IRIs -- confirming a bare '#' resolves against the SPARQL endpoint's own request URI, not the source document, exactly as Step 50 already warned. Fixed by rewriting every embedded SPARQL/Turtle example's PREFIX : <#> to an absolute PREFIX : <{source-url}/data#>, matching the ex: namespace already used for this document's materialized instance data (ex:alice, ex:AcmeCorp, etc.) so INSERT and SELECT queries are internally consistent and match real IRIs."@en .

:step2 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    schema:name "Verification method: curl status-code triage without performing unauthorized writes"@en ;
    schema:text "Anonymous SPARQL UPDATE (INSERT DATA) on linkeddata.uriburner.com returns 401 (WWW-Authenticate: Digest) for syntactically VALID queries, and 400 with a SPARQL compiler parse error for genuinely malformed ones -- confirmed with a deliberately broken control query. This makes 401-vs-400 a safe, no-write way to confirm 13 INSERT DATA query bodies parse correctly, without needing credentials or performing an actual write to a shared public endpoint. Read-only SELECT/CONSTRUCT queries (6 of the 19) were run fully (200 OK, results inspected) since they have no side effects."@en .

:step3 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    schema:name "Found and fixed a real SPARQL syntax bug: TRIPLE() inside a CONSTRUCT template's object position"@en ;
    schema:text "CONSTRUCT { :provenanceReport rdf:reifies TRIPLE(?person, :worksFor, ?org) ; ... } raised 'syntax error at TRIPLE before (' on Virtuoso 08.03.3335, even though TRIPLE() works fine as a SELECT/BIND expression. Fix: BIND(TRIPLE(?person, :worksFor, ?org) AS ?stmt) in WHERE, then reference ?stmt in the CONSTRUCT template -- verified 200 OK."@en .

:step4 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    schema:name "Found two genuine Virtuoso 08.03.3335 engine limitations (not documentation bugs) -- documented honestly rather than silently worked around"@en ;
    schema:text "(1) A triple term used directly as a subject -- << :alice :worksFor :AcmeCorp >> :assertedBy :bob . -- and a nested triple term -- << << ... >> :statedBy ... >> :confidence 0.9 . -- both parse cleanly (reach the 401 auth gate) but fail at INSERT execution time with an internal 'Bad argument to iri_to_id (), type 189' storage error. The rdf:reifies << ... >> pattern (triple term only ever appearing as the OBJECT of rdf:reifies) works fine. (2) VALUES ?stmt { << :alice :worksFor :AcmeCorp >> } raises a genuine SPARQL compiler syntax error; the TRIPLE() function-call form was also tried as an alternative inside VALUES and rejected identically. Both examples were kept in the companion RDF/HTML/MD as spec-accurate SPARQL 1.2 syntax, with schema:description on each schema:SoftwareSourceCode entity stating the live-verified engine behavior and date, rather than deleted or silently rewritten to hide the limitation."@en .

:step5 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    schema:name "General principle: 'verify the sample queries' after loading data to UB means test against the real endpoint, not re-read the TTL"@en ;
    schema:text "Re-parsing/re-validating the companion TTL locally would not have caught either defect class (relative-IRI resolution and engine-specific syntax/execution limits only manifest against the live SPARQL compiler). When a user says data is loaded and asks for verification, run the actual queries against the actual endpoint before declaring the workbench correct."@en .

:step6 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    schema:name "User pointed out INSERT will always fail anonymously (ACL) — isolate whether a live-test failure is query-form-specific before generalizing"@en ;
    schema:text "User asked whether the VALUES-with-triple-term failure was SELECT-specific. Tested the identical VALUES ?stmt { << ... >> } pattern across ASK, SELECT (inline WHERE and trailing VALUES), and CONSTRUCT, plus a control VALUES with a plain IRI (which worked in all forms) — same parser error reproduced in every query form, confirming the limitation is general to the VALUES clause accepting a triple-term constant, not tied to SELECT. Corrected the schema:description on :ceValuesTripleTerm to state this precisely instead of leaving an implicit SELECT-scoped claim."@en .

:step7 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 7 ;
    schema:name "'Remove INSERTs from the doc' — elicited scope (all 13 vs. just the 11 I invented) before deleting real post content"@en ;
    schema:text "Since anonymous SPARQL UPDATE always 401s on this endpoint, the user asked to remove INSERT DATA examples from the live SPARQL Workbench. Asked via AskUserQuestion whether this included the post's own 2 genuine INSERT DATA examples (its actual 'SPARQL 1.2 Query Support' section) or only the 11 I had artificially wrapped in INSERT DATA to get Turtle-only post content into the workbench — genuine ambiguity since deleting real post content vs. just my own invented wrapping have very different content-completeness consequences. User chose 'remove all 13'. Reverted the 11 to their original bare-Turtle form (schema:programmingLanguage 'Turtle', dropped from the workbench filter); kept the post's 2 genuine INSERT DATA examples' text verbatim but relabeled schema:programmingLanguage to 'SPARQL Update' (accurate — INSERT DATA is SPARQL Update, not SPARQL Query or Turtle) specifically so they fall outside the generator's exact 'SPARQL' language-string filter and no longer render as live-workbench accordions, while remaining fully documented as reference text. Net: workbench dropped from 19 to 6 accordions, all read-only and either verified-working or honestly-documented as a known engine limitation."@en .

:step8 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 8 ;
    schema:name "Missed a step: verified 401-vs-400 status against urn:graph:employees but never redirected the 5 remaining live queries' GRAPH clause to the actual DAV graph"@en ;
    schema:text "User pasted a URIBurner query link that returned an empty solution and asked why. Root cause: after documenting that urn:graph:employees is empty (since anonymous INSERT can never populate it), the fix should have been to point the 5 GRAPH-scoped live workbench queries (17-21) at the actual DAV-uploaded named graph (https://linkeddata.uriburner.com/DAV/demos/daas/{stem}.ttl, per Step 41) where the 530 real triples live — this was verified but never applied to the query text itself. Fixed by adding a DAV_GRAPH constant to the generator script and rewriting GRAPH <urn:graph:employees> to GRAPH <{DAV_GRAPH}> in all 5. Live re-test: query 17 (TRIPLE() over plain :worksFor triples) now returns a real, non-empty, correctly-resolved result since ex:alice ex:worksFor ex:AcmeCorp is genuinely materialized there; queries 18/19/20 correctly return 0 rows (documented: materialized data uses the ont:reifiesStatementAbout RDF-1.1-safe stand-in, not real rdf:reifies << >>, because the local generator's rdflib-based parser has no RDF-star support) — 0 rows is now the CORRECT, explained outcome for those three, not a wrong-graph bug. Left the post's 2 genuine INSERT DATA reference examples (13/14, SPARQL Update, not live) pointed at the post's own urn:graph:employees verbatim, since they are quoted documentation text, not live queries."@en .

:step9 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 9 ;
    schema:name "Found a real generator-level bug: the shared 'Run live' link builder hardcodes text/x-html+tr for every SELECT, which breaks on TRIPLE()-function results"@en ;
    schema:text "User pasted the (now correctly DAV-graph-scoped) query 17 link and it still 400'd. Isolated the cause with minimal repros: a triple term built via the << >> literal syntax renders fine in text/x-html+tr, but the same value built via BIND(TRIPLE(...) AS ?x) fails identically with or without GRAPH, with literal or variable arguments -- error is 'Virtuoso 22023 SR066: Unsupported case in CONVERT (ARRAY_OF_POINTER -> VARCHAR)' inside DB.DBA.SPARQL_RESULTS_HTML_TR_WRITE. Both forms work fine in application/sparql-results+json. Checked whether an inline '#output-format:' query pragma could override the URL format= param -- it cannot, the URL param always wins. Root cause in the generator itself: rdf-infographic-skill/scripts/templates/base_template.html's shared updateLiveLink() JS hardcoded format=text%2Fx-html%2Btr for every SELECT regardless of content (also never branched CONSTRUCT/DESCRIBE to text/x-html-nice-turtle, though that combination happens to still return 200 on this build). Fixed generator-wide: updateLiveLink() now regex-detects /\\bTRIPLE\\s*\\(/i in the query text and routes to application/sparql-results+json instead, and properly branches CONSTRUCT/DESCRIBE to text/x-html-nice-turtle. Verified end-to-end: simulated the 'Execute' button loading query 17 into the shared textarea in a real browser, confirmed the resulting #sparqlLiveLink href now carries format=application%2Fsparql-results%2Bjson, and curl-tested that exact URL returns 200. Also patched build_md.py's live_url() with the same TRIPLE()-detection so the Markdown companion's live links match. This is a fix to the shared rdf-infographic-skill template, so it benefits every future document generated with this skill, not just this one."@en .

:step10 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 10 ;
    schema:name "RDF-star supplement graph IRIs -- DAV file PUT always creates a graph named after the file's OWN DAV URL, never the target graph you intend"@en ;
    schema:text "Main companion graph (rdflib-safe, ont:reifiesStatementAbout stand-in, 530 triples): https://linkeddata.uriburner.com/DAV/demos/daas/rdf-1-2-support-in-virtuoso-claude_sonnet_5-1.ttl . Supplement file with genuine rdf:reifies << >> / {| |} triples, delivered separately (not rdflib-parseable) at rdf-1-2-support-in-virtuoso-claude_sonnet_5-1-rdfstar-supplement.ttl: when the user uploaded it via a plain DAV PUT, Virtuoso auto-created a SEPARATE graph named after that file's own DAV URL -- https://linkeddata.uriburner.com/DAV/demos/daas/rdf-1-2-support-in-virtuoso-claude_sonnet_5-1-rdfstar-supplement.ttl (confirmed live: 11 triples landed there, 0 in the main graph) -- rather than merging into the main graph as intended. First fix attempt: authenticated SPARQL 1.1 Update 'ADD <supplement-graph> TO <main-graph>' via /sparql-auth (merges, non-destructive). Lesson: any DAV-PUT-based load always creates/targets a graph keyed to the uploaded resource's own URL; reaching a DIFFERENT existing graph requires the SPARQL 1.1 Graph Store Protocol (POST with an explicit ?graph-uri= query parameter) or a follow-up ADD/COPY/MOVE SPARQL Update, never a bare DAV PUT of a differently-named file. Superseded by Step 11's simpler approach."@en .

:step11 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 11 ;
    schema:name "Simpler than a separate supplement graph: maintain a DEPLOY variant with the same DAV filename, re-PUT it, skip the Graph Store Protocol entirely"@en ;
    schema:text "User asked why not just merge the RDF-star content into the original TTL for re-upload, rather than a separate supplement file. Correct instinct -- the constraint was conflated: rdflib (used by the LOCAL generator pipeline) cannot parse RDF-star, but the file the USER uploads to Virtuoso does not have to be the same file the generator reads. Fix: keep the generator-input file (rdf-1-2-support-in-virtuoso-claude_sonnet_5-1.ttl) untouched and rdflib-parseable, and produce a second DEPLOY-only variant (rdf-1-2-support-in-virtuoso-claude_sonnet_5-1-DEPLOY.ttl = generator-input content + the RDF-star supplement triples appended, using the file's OWN already-declared ex: prefix rather than redeclaring ':' to a second, conflicting namespace) for the user to PUT over the SAME DAV URL as before. A same-URL PUT overwrites the existing graph directly -- no ADD, no ?graph-uri=, no stray graph, exactly the single familiar upload step the user already knew. Verified the generator-input file still parses standalone (528 triples) after this split. Superseded by Step 12 -- user chose the single-file tradeoff instead."@en .

:step12 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 12 ;
    schema:name "Final decision: single canonical file, accept the local-regenerability tradeoff"@en ;
    schema:text "User explicitly chose one file over the generator-safe split: 'all the sample data should be in rdf-1-2-support-in-virtuoso-claude_sonnet_5-1.ttl, then I can delete -DEPLOY and -supplement.' Executed: copied the DEPLOY variant's content over the canonical filename (cp DEPLOY.ttl -> rdf-1-2-support-in-virtuoso-claude_sonnet_5-1.ttl), deleted both the -DEPLOY.ttl and -rdfstar-supplement.ttl files, leaving exactly one artifact (49349 bytes, 769 lines) at the canonical stem. Consequence flagged to the user: this file is no longer rdflib-parseable (contains real << >>/rdf:reifies/{| |} syntax), so any FUTURE regeneration of the HTML/MD from this exact file will fail at the local generator step; regenerating would require stripping the appended RDF-star block first, then re-appending it (or re-fetching the deployed graph's RDF-1.1-compatible subset) afterward. Upload command simplified to a plain same-name PUT since local and remote filenames now match exactly. Lesson: when a user prioritizes 'one file matches what's deployed' over 'stays regenerable by my local tooling', honor that tradeoff directly rather than re-arguing for the split -- but flag the consequence once, clearly, so it doesn't surprise a later regeneration request."@en .

:step13 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 13 ;
    schema:name "Use the live Virtuoso/UB SPARQL endpoint itself as the RDF 1.2/RDF-star syntax-validation oracle, not just local rdflib"@en ;
    schema:text "User asked whether UB could substitute for rdflib when rdflib can't parse RDF-star, and whether a newer rdflib version might already support RDF 1.2. Checked: rdflib 7.6.0 (latest on PyPI, confirmed via 'pip index versions rdflib') still registers no turtle-star/RDF-star parser plugin at all -- confirmed empirically too (a bare << >> triple fails BadSyntax in the notation3 parser). So the answer is yes, use UB: wrap any candidate RDF 1.2/RDF-star Turtle fragment (including {| |} annotation syntax) in 'ASK { <fragment> }' and submit it anonymously via GET to https://linkeddata.uriburner.com/sparql -- a 200 with a true/false JSON boolean means Virtuoso's own compiler accepted the syntax; a 400 with a SPARQL compiler error means it did not. Zero write risk (ASK against unbound data has no side effects), zero auth needed. Concretely validated the exact RDF-star block appended to rdf-1-2-support-in-virtuoso-claude_sonnet_5-1.ttl this way (200, true) as a formal confirmation, retroactively covering ad hoc individual-snippet probes already used earlier in this session (Steps 1-9) without being named as a general technique. Going forward: for any RDF 1.2/RDF-star content destined for this or another Virtuoso-backed endpoint, validate via this ASK-wrap technique BEFORE delivery, rather than caveat-ing content as 'not locally verifiable' -- local rdflib is the wrong tool for this class of content; the target engine is the right one, and it is reachable anonymously and safely for syntax checks."@en .
